ge)\n    # compute message text\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    ...\n```\nBut avoid mutating record.message? Existing code uses record.message for prefix detection. Original logging sets record.message; we could compute message = record.getMessage() ourselves without setting record.message attribute (fine).\n\nLet's write:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if (\n            budget is not None\n            and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", False) is not True\n            and len(message) > budget\n        ):\n            keep = budget - len(_OMIT_MARKER)\n            head = (keep + 1) // 2\n            tail = keep - head\n            message = message[:head] + _OMIT_MARKER + (message[-tail:] if tail > 0 else \"\")\n            record = _copy_record_with_message(record, message)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        return self._decorate(record, msg)\n```\n\nFor the copy: use `copy.copy(record)` then set msg/args. But copy module import allowed? Imports list allowed: collections, datetime, itertools, json, math, queue, random, re, stat, statistics, time, unicodedata. copy not allowed! Hmm, allowed imports for my action code, not for business source. Business source can import anything. But careful — the source restriction is about my code? \"Imports allowed\" refers to my action snippets. In the business file I can add imports freely (standard library allowed: \"日志模块只扩展格式化器和默认初始化，可增加必要标准库导入或辅助函数\").\n\nAlternative without copy: temporarily set record.msg and record.args then restore in try/finally. That mutates momentarily but restores. Safer to use copy.copy from stdlib. Actually simplest: manually construct the record copy via a new object:\n\n```python\ndef _truncate_record(record, message):\n    clone = copy.copy(record)\n    clone.msg = message\n    clone.args = ()\n    clone.exc_info = None? \n```\nBut we want to 